guns are an inescapable part of video  games  in fact they feature promptly in movies  tv shows comic books and  pretty much every type of media going  no matter your stance them socially or  politically they can be a tool to  introduce action suspense  drama and intensity especially into a  video game  ever since 1992's wolfenstein 3d the  grandfather of the fps genre  developers have tried to create weapons  that balance realism satisfaction and  various other considerations  with what the technology at the time  could achieve now we all know how  frequent the site of a firearm is in  video games little  gun punfia there but how do they  actually work how do guns and bullet  physics change between games  how does the bullet go from here to here  the most common type of shooter  mechanics is hitscan this technique uses  technology similar to the one that first  allowed 3d games to be developed  raycasting raycasting was used to render  a three-dimensional scene into a  two-dimensional image  that could be displayed on your screen  rays would be traced from the virtual  eye of the player through each pixel in  the virtual space  calculating things like the color and  light of the object visible through it  to ensure it rendered correctly in the  game world the algorithm was made up of  a bunch of complex mathematics and  geometry but the calculations could be  done relatively quickly  which was perfect for first person  shooter games especially when resources  and computing power was fairly limited  take wolfenstein 3d for example it's  built from a square based grid with  walls being a uniform height  to render the world onto the screen a  ray is traced for each column of visible  pixels  the algorithm can then scale each object  to the correct size depending on how  long the ray takes to  intersect or hit each object  [Music]  scan weapons work in a very similar way  to raycasting and have done even as far  back as the previous example of  wolfenstein 3d  instead of a wide array originating from  the player's eye a more narrow one will  be cast from the barrel of a weapon  being fired  on the most basic level it will  determine where a weapon is being  pointed  use that information to generate a ray  and then using the same raycasting idea  of if a ray is intersecting with  something  to determine if that object is being hit  on a hit the game engine will then enact  the desired result  rendering damage in the environment or  to an enemy and initializing the correct  sound effects and logic  the cause and effect of the player  clicking their mouse or pulling a  trigger and the bullet hitting its  target all takes place instantly  and while that's obviously not how real  world weapons work game designers use  clever lighting graphics and  sounds to make the gunfire look and feel  as realistic and satisfying as possible  after game designers have nailed these  fundamentals they can go on to add  things like bullets ricocheting by  programming rays to bounce off surfaces  or adding bullet penetration by allowing  the ray to continue traveling through  each target  up to a set distance another method to  mimic realism was to add variables to  the angle or placement of the ray  which would give a sense of bullet  spread from wolfenstein 3d  doom and goldeneye to more modern games  like call of duty overwatch and  counter-strike  most weapons in fps games use this  method of implementing their gunfire  hit scan games tend to be a little more  accessible as you're not asking someone  who just picks up a game to start  learning things like  bullet drop or lead times the learning  curve is a little more shallow but it  does mean you get instant gratification  from utilizing your virtual armory but  there's also another method that's a  little more grounded in realism  that many shooters use to create a more  immersive experience  games that want their simulated weapons  to be as close as possible to the real  world counterparts  are more likely to implement a physical  projectile system rather than computing  shots fired with a detecting  intersecting rays a new object is  spawned with each round fired from the  weapon  as the bullets exist as a real entity in  the game world it means that they can  have their own values and behavior  each shot has its own mass and velocity  with the potential of in-game systems  influencing each individual bullet  things like gravity and wind can start  to affect the flight path of your lethal  payload with this system  but it comes at the cost of requiring  extra computing power  since each object will have its own  behavior and logic applied to it  the game engine as well as the game  client and any multiplayer servers  must make use of both raw computing  speed and clever workarounds  to ensure the game experience remains  consistent between each player  games that use this method will tend to  have a slightly steeper learning curve  and a higher skill ceiling  because obviously they need to take into  consideration things like bullet drop  and lead time  of course depending on how far they  choose to engage two of the most popular  titles that predominantly use physical  instances of bullets are pubg  and the battlefield series games that  boast a more realistic combat experience  and two franchises that i personally  really enjoy but even i can admit they  aren't the easiest to get  into for first-time players but game  designers don't have to stick with one  technique or the other  in fact it's pretty commonplace to have  both techniques merged together into the  same game  take fortnite for example for the most  part the weapons are hitscan which lends  itself to the faster  paced close combat gameplay experience  but then their sniper rifles use a  physical projectile system  players that take the time to consider  bullet drop and compensate for a moving  target  are rewarded with a high damage attack  from a potentially safe range  it's also extremely common to see games  that have weapons like rocket launchers  and bows  use a physics system for those while the  rest of the arsenal will be hit scanned  overwatch is another example of this  combined system soldier 76 in particular  his assault rifle is hit scan but then  the helix rocket he can also fire is  physical but the mechanic a developer  chooses will depend on its audience  the genre of its game and the technical  restrictions it's under  but what's most interesting is that  while shooters look and play so vastly  different from those from the ps1  n64 and even ms-dos they still have very  similar central mechanics and ideas at  their core  these are mechanics that have been  refined and improved over time of course  but fundamentally  they are still very much grounded in the  first iterations of these ideas  but i suppose you know what they say if  aim broke don't fix it  [Music]  but for now at least you should have a  better understanding on how the enemy  team keeps mowing you down  but would you prefer the instant  gratification of hitscan or taking the  time to learn the physics system of a  ballistic based fps  let me know in the comments section  below or you can find me on twitter at  irregular dave  and until next time thank you so much  for watching  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  you
 

